"""Render the descriptive replication report from checked analysis artifacts."""
import json,html
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]; D=R/'docs'; data=json.load(open(D/'data/replication-comparison.json'))
names={'qwen':'Qwen3.6-27B','llama-base':'Llama-3.1-70B base','tulu':'Tulu-3-70B'}
def pct(x):return f'{100*x:.1f}%'
def pp(x):return f'{100*x:+.1f}'
def table(head,rows):return '<div style="overflow-x:auto"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+x+'</th>' for x in head)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+str(x)+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
p=[]
def para(s):p.append('<p>'+s+'</p>')
def title(s):p.append('<h2>'+s+'</h2>')
para('These are <strong>O-lens classifications</strong>, not J++ scores. The evidence supports role-related content in all three lenses and an Assist–Copy difference in Tulu. It does not establish that an Assistant perspective dominates user-token representations.')
title('What was run')
para('Each model received the same 26 user-message variants under Assist and Copy instructions: 52 conditions, including factual controls and crossed appraisals. Each model has 156 unique greedy readouts and 195 stochastic readouts (39 fixed sites × 5 seeds), for 351 primary readouts. These are repeated measurements of a small, deliberately selected scenario set, not 351 independent user turns.')
para('Base Llama and Tulu used identical Tulu-format input IDs, including the system instruction “You are an AI assistant.” Base behavior is therefore conditional on that role prompt. Activations were captured from separately tokenized prefixes ending exactly at the selected character boundary. Their trained O-lenses read after block 56 of 80; Qwen reads after block 45 of 64. Each uses its own trained lens and native injection method. Differences across lenses cannot be attributed solely to post-training.')
para('GPT-5.4-2026-03-05 labeled every phrase using the same frozen broad rubric and the available source prefix, without model names, paired outputs or main/control labels. Each new lens supplied 3,510 phrases. User includes implied user requests, goals, beliefs and feelings. Assistant includes judgments, reactions, answer openings and response plans. Labels can overlap or both be absent. A label is an attribution of decoded text, not a validated measurement of a model’s internal belief.')
title('At the final request boundary')
para('Presence means at least one labeled phrase in a readout. Density means the fraction of its phrases bearing the label, averaged equally across readouts. To avoid giving Qwen more chances to trigger a label through extra phrases, the table uses the first ten phrases for every model.')
p.append(table(['Model','User presence','Assistant presence','User density','Assistant density'],[[names[m],f"{s['request_first10']['presence']['user']}/52",f"{s['request_first10']['presence']['assistant']}/52 ({pct(s['request_first10']['presence']['assistant']/52)})",pct(s['request_first10']['mean_phrase_fraction']['user']),pct(s['request_first10']['mean_phrase_fraction']['assistant'])] for m,s in data.items()]))
para('All 52 readouts per model contain some User-labeled text. This does <em>not</em> mean all 52 explicitly model the user’s psychology: many phrases simply continue a request. Likewise, an Assistant label can be a generic answer heading. With all Qwen phrases retained, Assistant presence is 51/52 rather than 47/52, and Assistant density is 33.3% rather than 29.6%. Both Llama lenses produce exactly ten phrases per readout.')
title('Does the instruction change the readout?')
p.append(table(['Model','Assist: Assistant presence','Copy: Assistant presence','Assist: Assistant density','Copy: Assistant density'],[[names[m],f"{s['request_by_task']['assist']['presence']['assistant']}/26",f"{s['request_by_task']['copy']['presence']['assistant']}/26",pct(s['request_by_task']['assist']['mean_phrase_fraction']['assistant']),pct(s['request_by_task']['copy']['mean_phrase_fraction']['assistant'])] for m,s in data.items()]))
para('This table retains all phrases. Within each model, compare Assist and Copy for the same message. Tulu has a clear descriptive request-boundary contrast; base Llama’s contrast goes in the opposite direction. Assistant-labeled content under Copy alone is insufficient evidence of an automatic Assistant perspective: the content may be generic conversation scaffolding, and base Llama often fails the copying task.')
para('For a consistent position comparison, the following uses the same 22 message variants at all three anchors, excluding Tylenol’s special historical anchor and confidentiality’s merged boundary. Values are paired Assist minus Copy differences in Assistant phrase density, in percentage points; all phrases are retained. Positive values mean more Assistant-labeled content under Assist.')
p.append(table(['Model','Fact boundary','Appraisal boundary','Request boundary'],[[names[m]]+[pp(s['paired_matched_anchors'][a]['assistant']['mean_assist_minus_copy']) for a in ['fact','primary','request']] for m,s in data.items()]))
para('Tulu’s contrast is strongest at the request boundary; Qwen’s is strongest at the appraisal boundary. These are descriptive results on selected, dependent conditions, not population estimates or evidence that one lens measures perspective more accurately.')
title('What the controls actually show')
para('The examples below are illustrative checks, not a new exhaustive semantic score. Broad label counts alone cannot tell whether a readout correctly tracks the changed fact or appraisal.')
examples=[('tulu','tylenol-stated-assist-0__fact',[3,6,8]),('tulu','tylenol-stated-assist-1__fact',[1,3,8]),('tulu','arithmetic-stated-assist-0__primary',[1,3,7]),('tulu','arithmetic-stated-assist-1__primary',[1,3,6]),('llama-base','arithmetic-stated-assist-0__primary',[5,9]),('llama-base','arithmetic-stated-assist-1__primary',[4,10])]
for m,source,indices in examples:
 rows=json.load(open(D/f'data/models/{m}/analysis/readouts.json'));r=next(x for x in rows if x['source']==source)
 p.append('<details><summary>'+html.escape(names[m]+' · '+source)+'</summary><pre class="raw">'+html.escape(r['prefix'])+'</pre><ol>'+''.join('<li value="'+str(i)+'">'+html.escape(r['phrases'][i-1])+'</li>' for i in indices)+'</ol></details>')
para('In Tulu’s 8,000 mg Tylenol fact readout, phrases include “far above the recommended dosage” and “overdose”; these do not appear in the corresponding 1,000 mg readout. This is evidence of dose-sensitive hazard content. It does not by itself identify whose perspective owns that assessment: an overdose phrase can continue a user narrative. The 1,000 mg readout also invents nausea and a helper greeting, illustrating noise.')
para('For confident arithmetic, both correct and incorrect Tulu cases generate “Am I wrong? Please correct me if”. Thus a User label does not establish faithful representation of the stated confidence, and these readouts do not cleanly distinguish correct from incorrect arithmetic. Base readouts include both confident user continuations and generic helper openings in both conditions. More generally, response scaffolding and user continuation can coexist without resolving the intended perspective contrast.')
title('Review and sensitivity')
para('Codex reviewed all 52 request-boundary greedy readouts (520 phrases) for each new model, with prefixes and automated labels visible. This was not a human or independent blinded review. Six base and ten Tulu phrase corrections are recorded separately; the dashboard and primary tables retain the original GPT labels. Other anchors and stochastic samples were not exhaustively reviewed.')
p.append(table(['Model','Assistant presence: original → corrected','User density: original → corrected','Assistant density: original → corrected'],[[names[m],f"{data[m]['request']['presence']['assistant']}/52 → {data[m]['review_sensitivity']['request']['presence']['assistant']}/52",pct(data[m]['request']['mean_phrase_fraction']['user'])+' → '+pct(data[m]['review_sensitivity']['request']['mean_phrase_fraction']['user']),pct(data[m]['request']['mean_phrase_fraction']['assistant'])+' → '+pct(data[m]['review_sensitivity']['request']['mean_phrase_fraction']['assistant'])] for m in ['llama-base','tulu']]))
para('Corrections remove unrelated fragments such as incomplete questions about off-the-shoulder clothing and handle mixed request/answer phrases consistently. Tulu’s corrected Assistant presence increases from 43 to 44 because a mixed phrase gains that label; another phrase loses it elsewhere. Aggregate density remains unchanged. Ambiguous greetings, draft text and generic headings remain a source of uncertainty.')
p.append(table(['Model','Sites with Assistant in all 5 samples','Assistant presence agrees with greedy','Median Assistant density range across 5 samples'],[[names[m],str(s['samples']['assistant']['presence_count_out_of_five_histogram'].get('5',0))+'/39',str(s['samples']['assistant']['agreement_with_greedy'])+'/195',pct(s['samples']['assistant']['median_density_range'])] for m,s in data.items()]))
para('Assistant presence is much less stable across sampled decodes for the two Llama lenses than for Qwen. Five samples are repeated decodes of the same activation, not five independent experimental observations. High greedy presence should therefore not be read as robust identification at every site.')
para('All 164 named-anchor exact-repeat and fixed-length future-content substitution checks, and all 26 paired fixed-length shared-prefix checks, passed per new model. Changing sequence length nevertheless changes BF16 computation shapes: only 114/156 sensitivity decodes per model are token-identical to the primary readout; 42 differ. These diagnostic outputs are excluded from the primary analysis and were not exhaustively classified, so semantic or label stability under this numerical perturbation remains unresolved.')
para('For actual generated replies, Tulu exactly copies the complete message in 22/26 Copy conditions; base Llama does so in 0/26 complete outputs. However, base starts with an exact copy in 4/26 and then continues. The separate eight long base continuations all reach the 1,024-token cap and invent role headers; their original 256-token prefixes match. See the <a href="base-behavior.html">full serialized inputs and continuations</a>. Copy failure limits interpretation of the base Assist–Copy control.')
title('Interpretation and complete data')
para('The supported conclusion is that the lenses decode both user-related and assistant-related language during user-prefix processing. Tulu’s request readouts are instruction-sensitive, and some fact controls elicit specific evaluations. The current measurements do not separate faithful psychological modeling from continuation prediction, establish an Assistant default independent of prompting, or justify ranking the models by “Assistant privilege.” No causal intervention here establishes that these decoded attributions drive behavior.')
para('<a href="data/replication-comparison.json">All computed statistics and source hashes</a> · <a href="analysis.html">Earlier Qwen analysis</a>')
for m in ['llama-base','tulu']:
 root='data/models/'+m+'/analysis/'
 para(names[m]+': '+ ' · '.join('<a href="'+root+f+'">'+label+'</a>' for f,label in [('readouts.json','351 readouts'),('broad-perspectives.json','original GPT labels'),('broad-perspective-provenance.json','label provenance'),('request-review.json','review and corrections'),('summary.json','statistics')]))
(D/'replication-analysis.html').write_text('''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>O-lens model comparison</title><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=IBM+Plex+Serif:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"><link href="style.css" rel="stylesheet"><style>main{max-width:1050px}h2{font-size:20px;margin-top:32px}p{line-height:1.65}td,th{font-size:14px}details{margin:12px 0}.raw{white-space:pre-wrap}</style></head><body><header><h1>O-lens <span>· model comparison</span></h1><a href="index.html">Back to dashboard</a></header><main>'''+''.join(p)+'</main></body></html>')
